<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kewu Plain</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Prambanan Plain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kewu Plain, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prambanan">Prambanan</a> Plain, is a fertile area in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, on the southern slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-merapi">Mount Merapi</a> near Yogyakarta. In the eighth and ninth centuries it was a heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medang-kingdom">Medang</a> kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendra dynasty</a>, and it holds a dense group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist temples, including Prambanan, Sewu and Plaosan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kewu-plain.jpg" alt="Kewu Plain" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BPCB Jawa Tengah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_of_Sajiwan-Prambanan_temples.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The plain, also called the Opak River Valley, is a fertile volcanic lowland lying between the Merapi-Merbabu complex to the north, the Bantul lowlands and the Sewu karst limestone range to the south, the Bengawan Solo valley to the east, the Progo River to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedu-plain">Kedu Plain</a> to the north-west. It falls within the Yogyakarta Special Region and the Sleman and Klaten regencies and reaches towards Solo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>.</p><p>For centuries its rich volcanic soil supported intensive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming. The early Mataram kingdom depended on rice yields and on taxes collected from its subjects, scenes of rice cultivation appear in the reliefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prambanan">Prambanan</a>, and many paddy fields of the landscape remain much as they were.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Known in the past as Mamratipura, the plain was the centre of the Mataram kingdom from the 8th to the 10th century and later of the Mataram Sultanate in the 16th century. It holds some of the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. The Kalasan inscription names the Buddhist temple of Kalasan, built early in the 8th century to honour the bodhisattva Tara, as the oldest on the plain. The Sewu complex, whose original name was Manjusrigrha, was a royal Buddhist temple and is older than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prambanan">Prambanan</a>.</p><p>The great 9th-century Shivaic complex of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prambanan">Prambanan</a>, dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trimurti">Trimurti</a>, probably marked a return of royal patronage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> under the Mataram kings. Plaosan, probably of the 9th century, is thought to have been built by a Hindu king for his Buddhist queen, and Sojiwan was probably the mortuary temple of the Buddhist queen Pramodhawardhani. Several temples were buried by volcanic deposits: Sambisari, found in 1966 under 4 m of lahar, and Kedulan, found by sand diggers in 1994.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prambanan">Prambanan</a>, the plain and the hills around it hold the temples of Bubrah, Lumbung, Sari, Banyunibo, Barong, Ijo and Gebang and the hilltop compound of Ratu Boko. In 2012 the Central Java Heritage Preservation Authority proposed treating an area of 30 square kilometres around Prambanan as a protected sanctuary, on the model of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> archaeological area in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, with limits on new buildings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kewu Plain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prambanan was built in the 9th century in the <b>Prambanan Plain</b> of southern Java, where the court and urban centre of the Mataram kingdom lay nearby.</blockquote><a href="/work/prambanan">Prambanan · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
